#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3428605-2071-5dfd-af29-d6ef0c96c0af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103007} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d9d7a0-e9aa-5f50-8538-06afaaab65a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103008} {:answer/id #uuid "0d43707b-ee5a-57af-b124-47eb278a0791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103009} {:answer/id #uuid "27c5a236-3060-583a-9123-c978ad354f12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103010} {:answer/id #uuid "606e69dd-42f4-5d0a-8bd3-3c8466a094df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d43707b-ee5a-57af-b124-47eb278a0791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103009} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7f19366-c132-5008-bf78-90ea03a83a40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37b4a96f-ccf7-5874-8bcd-7955ee9c3b7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-22810" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ defined for $x\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$   on $(0,2\\pi )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points  $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\pi ,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{5\\pi }{3},2\\pi )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |  | $\\pi $ |  | $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
